---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const steps = [
  { n: "01", t: "Shade",    s: ["Covered for the last", "20 to 40 days"] },
  { n: "02", t: "Pick",     s: ["Youngest leaves,", "first harvest only"] },
  { n: "03", t: "Steam",    s: ["Within hours,", "to stop oxidation"] },
  { n: "04", t: "Dry",      s: ["Cooled, then", "dried flat"] },
  { n: "05", t: "Destem",   s: ["Stem and vein out.", "This is tencha"] },
  { n: "06", t: "Grind",    s: ["Granite, slowly.", "30 to 40g an hour"] },
];

const essays = [
  {
    title: "My Lifelong Career as a Reader and Writer",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 6, page 26",
    href: "/writing/reader-and-writer/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2026/03/Translingual-Issue-6.pdf",
    note: "Reading has blown to my lost soul a breath of fresh air, filling my vain mind with the generosity of language.",
  },
  {
    title: "A Film Analysis: Grave of the Fireflies",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, page 16",
    href: "/writing/grave-of-the-fireflies/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2023/04/Translingual-V1.-I2.-Final.pdf",
    note: "In the dark shelter where the siblings live, fireflies serve as their sole lighting resource, and possibly implied further as a beam of hope in the darkest phases of life.",
  },
  {
    title: "Shades of Black",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, page 42",
    href: "/writing/shades-of-black/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2023/04/Translingual-V1.-I2.-Final.pdf",
    note: "Putting on a solid black jacket most of the time has left people with an impression of a tough person who would never be disturbed by spare emotions.",
  },
  {
    title: "Imprisonment in The Allegory of the Cave and The House of Asterion",
    where: "Unpublished",
    href: "/writing/imprisonment/",
    note: "I believe that the opposite of life is not death, but to exist without feeling alive.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Rough to Cut"
  description="The inspirations behind this site. Matcha as a morning ritual, semi-precious stones, and four essays. By Linh (Min) Le."
>
  <article class="rough">

    <header class="band g-cream rough-head">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Beyond work</p>
        <h1>Rough to Cut</h1>
        <p class="rough-lede">
          Curious about inspirations behind this site? Green and soft cream palettes running through
          every page. Fragments that form into a polished stone at first landing. And
          longer-than-necessary narratives under each work. They are intentional, yes, guided by
          instinct and shaped by fragments of what interests me outside of work.
        </p>
        <p class="rough-sub">
          Nothing professional below the line. Each piece, still, is a piece of my identity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell rough-split">
        <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Matcha</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>A ritual I have never wanted to shorten</h2>


        <p>
          Before anything else, twenty minutes. The bowl and the whisk live in the same corner of
          the kitchen and I go through the same sequence every morning, which is most of why it
          works. The day is still ahead of me, and for those twenty minutes the only thing
          asking anything of me is the bowl.
        </p>
        <p>
          I let the water stand off the boil for a minute, because full heat scorches the powder
          and it comes back bitter. I sift, which is the step I could talk myself out of on any
          given morning and the one that decides whether the surface goes smooth or grainy. Then
          the whisk, quick, in a narrow W, until the foam comes up fine and even across the bowl.
          Within a few seconds I can feel whether I am actually here or only moving my wrist, and
          on the mornings I hurried it, the bowl tells me so. By the time I put it down my head is
          quiet. That is the whole reason this routine outlasted every other one I have tried to
          keep.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I love is how far back the care goes. It starts in a field months earlier, and every
          step from there belongs to someone who took their time. The plants are shaded for the last
          twenty to forty days before harvest, and losing the light pushes them to make more
          chlorophyll and to turn their catechins into L-theanine, which is where both the colour
          and the sweetness come from. Only the youngest leaves are taken, steamed within hours so
          they cannot oxidise, dried, then stripped of stem and vein until only leaf is left. That
          is tencha. It becomes matcha between two granite stones, at roughly thirty grams an hour,
          because grinding faster heats the powder and the aroma goes with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Thirty grams an hour. I think about that standing there with my bowl. Whatever I add is
          the last and smallest part of something that has been meticulous for months, and I like
          being the end of it rather than the whole of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then there is what it does to me. The L-theanine comes with the caffeine and takes the
          edge off it, so I get alert without the jitters and stay that way for hours, with no
          crash at the end. It is gentle on an empty stomach. Coffee gives me a fast, restless hour
          and then drops me flat by early afternoon.
        </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img src="/media/rough/matcha-whisk.jpg" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="A bamboo chasen lifted out of a white ceramic bowl, matcha whisked to a flat green surface." />
          <figcaption>The whisk is bamboo, cut into a hundred tines, and it is the only tool in the process I own.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-leaf" style="--plate: var(--g-ivory);">
      <div class="shell rough-split mirror">
        <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Stones</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>A beauty the price tag never touched</h2>


        <p>
          I collect in seasons. Some months I am in and out of shops, other months I am not
          thinking about stones at all, and the collection grows in bursts.
        </p>
        <p>
          One of the first places I found after moving to Cincinnati was GeoRarities, a rock shop
          tucked into a suite inside an art studio building, the kind of place you would walk past
          without seeing it was there. Most of what I own came from small shops like that one.
        </p>
        <p>
          I collect semi-precious stones, and what one costs has never been the part that interests
          me. What I want to know is where it formed, how long that took, and what people have made
          of it over a few thousand years. My element in Eastern philosophy is Water, so what I
          pick up is nearly always blue or white.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lapis lazuli is my favourite piece. It is a rock made of several minerals at once.
          Lazurite gives the blue, calcite runs through it in thin white veins, and pyrite scatters
          across it in flecks of gold. The Egyptians set it into burial masks and carved it for
          royalty, and centuries later it was ground into ultramarine, a blue so costly that
          painters reserved it for the most important figure on the panel. It has carried the name
          stone of wisdom since the pharaohs.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is easy to confuse with sodalite, which is nearly the same blue under a shop&rsquo;s
          indoor lighting. The difference shows in sunlight. Sodalite carries almost no pyrite, so it
          stays flat, while lapis catches the light and throws back small gold sparks.
        </p>
        <p>
          The part that stays with me is the second half of what it is supposed to mean. Most descriptions of
          lapis stop at clear thinking. The tradition goes further and ties that deep blue to the
          throat, to speech, to saying a true thing in a way that is believed. That distinction is
          the one I care about in my own work. Reaching a clear answer is the part I can do alone.
          Carrying it to someone who has to act on it, in language that persuades rather than
          merely reports, is the harder half, and it is the half I keep working at.
        </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img src="/media/rough/lapis-lazuli.jpg" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Tumbled lapis lazuli stones, deep blue with white calcite veining and small flecks of gold pyrite." />
          <figcaption>Blue lazurite, white calcite, and the gold pyrite that tells it apart from sodalite.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Writing</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I learned to write before I learned to speak</h2>
        <p>
          A spoken thought has to leave the moment it forms, still half made, and it gets answered
          before I have finished having it. I have never been quick that way, and at some point I
          stopped trying to be.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the page every word gets time. I can weigh it, lift it out, set a truer one in its
          place, and keep going until what is written is what I meant, with nobody watching me do
          it. Whoever reads it gets only what survived that, which is the closest I come to being
          understood exactly.
        </p>

        <ol class="essays">
          {essays.map((e) => (
            <li class="essay">
              <h3 class="essay-title"><a href={e.href}>{e.title}</a></h3>
              <p class="essay-where figure">
                {e.where}
                {e.published && (
                  <> &middot; <a href={e.published} target="_blank" rel="noopener">as published</a></>
                )}
              </p>
              <blockquote class="essay-note"><p>{e.note}</p></blockquote>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band g-leaf rough-end">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p>
          That is what the hours outside work go to. If you came in from a case study, the
          rest of the site is one click back.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-out">
          <a class="cta ghost" href="/#work">Back to the work</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .rough-head { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .rough-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .rough-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin-top: .9rem;
    padding-top: .9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
  .rough-lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .rough-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 23rem);
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 3.25rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .rough-split.mirror { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 23rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .rough-split.mirror .shot { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
  .rough-split.mirror .prose { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; }
  .rough-split .shot { position: sticky; top: 5.5rem; margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .rough-split, .rough-split.mirror { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .rough-split.mirror .shot, .rough-split .shot { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; position: static; }
    .rough-split.mirror .prose { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; }
    .rough-split .shot { max-width: 34rem; margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  }

  .prose { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 1.15rem + 0.82vw, 1.85rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -.015em;
    margin: .3rem 0 1.3rem;
    max-width: none;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.05em; line-height: 1.62; }
  .rough-end { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .rough-end .prose p { max-width: 52ch; }
  .prose .beat {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 60, "WONK" 1;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .shot { margin: 1.8rem 0 2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .7rem; }
  .shot img {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .shot figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    padding-left: .75rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-line);
  }

  .fig { margin: 1.6rem 0 1.8rem; }
  .fig-frame {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: .8rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .fig-frame svg { display: block; width: 100%; min-width: 34rem; height: auto; }
  .st rect { fill: none; stroke: var(--rule-strong); stroke-width: 1; }
  .st.on rect { fill: var(--accent-wash); stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .sn  { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .1em; }
  .stt { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; fill: var(--ink); }
  .st.on .stt { fill: var(--accent-deep); }
  .sss { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 7.5px; fill: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cap { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .arw { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.2; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }

  .essays { list-style: none; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .essay { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .essay:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }
  .essay-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 40;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .essay-title a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent-line); }
  .essay-title a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .essay-where {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .05em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .essay-note { margin-top: .6rem; padding-left: .9rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-line); }
  .essay-note p {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 50, "WONK" 1;
    font-style: italic; font-size: var(--step-0); color: var(--accent-deep);
    line-height: 1.5; margin: 0;
  }

  .closing-out { margin-top: .8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; text-decoration: none;
    background: var(--accent-deep); color: var(--g-ivory);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-deep); border-radius: 7px;
    padding: .6rem .95rem; display: inline-block;
    transition: background .25s ease, border-color .25s ease, color .25s ease;
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--ink); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 34%, transparent); }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--accent-wash); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .cta:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
</style>
